WFBF: literal grid mode (Algorithm 1, beta=1, faithful) vs corner-point mode, n = 18-22.
This is a V0 run: wfbf() takes only geometry (bins, items from mi.read_instance), never weight,
real CoM or load-bearing data - wfbf.py's rank function substitutes an item's geometric centre for
the CoM term, exactly because "V0 has no CoM data" (see its docstring). No attach_v1_data /
attach_v2_data step is involved here, unlike the V1/V2 result tables this file is referenced from.
seed=1. 10 instances per size, run in the foreground (a backgrounded run stalled on this machine
and was killed; results below are from direct foreground execution, wall-clock timed per instance).

The enumeration cost this file measures (position count = H*W*L/beta) depends only on bin volume
and beta, not on which submodel is active, so the grid-vs-corner timing gap applies unchanged to
the WFBF rows quoted in the V1 and V2 result tables - only the OBJECTIVE would differ under V1/V2
data, and WFBF does not compute a V1/V2-aware objective at all (see Results_V2_n18-22 for its
7/50 V2 constraint violations).

WHY TWO MODES EXIST (see wfbf.py docstring)
--------------------------------------------
Algorithm 1, line 15, enumerates EVERY position on the beta-grid: H*W*L/beta candidates per
item per rotation. On the paper's own Physical Internet dataset, item/bin dimensions share large
common factors, so beta is coarse and literal enumeration is cheap. On instances regenerated by
our own generator (see Results_V1_n18-22 / Results_V2_n18-22), beta = gcd(item sides) is almost
always 1: for a 120x120x120 bin this is 1,728,000 candidate positions per item per rotation,
which is why "corner" mode (restricting candidates to the origin and the corners opened by
already-placed items - the same extreme-point idea the beam search uses) was used as the default
in the V1/V2 comparison tables. This file quantifies exactly what that default costs and saves.

