those runs; the earlier objective values are unaffected, since mode is orthogonal to correctness,
but the earlier WFBF TIME columns should be read as "corner-mode approximation", not "literal
Algorithm 1".

QUALITY: grid (literal) is much WORSE than corner here, not just slower
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
obj 0.6648 (grid) vs 0.2370 (corner) - corner mode wins by a wide margin, the opposite of what
"more faithful" might suggest. Two things combine to cause this:
  1. Algorithm 1's rank prefers low z and axis-proximity, evaluated over EVERY position; ties are
     broken by iteration order over the enumerated grid, which does not coincide with the
     bottom-up, corner-seeking order corner mode produces implicitly.
  2. corner mode restricts candidates to positions that are geometrically load-bearing by
     construction (origin, or a corner opened by a placed box), which happens to bias toward
     tightly-packed configurations; grid mode is free to select low-rank positions that leave
     unusable gaps, since nothing in the rank function penalises fragmentation directly.
This is worth stating plainly: for these regenerated instances, corner mode is not merely a
faster approximation of Algorithm 1, it produces BETTER packings than the literal grid, because
of how the two interact with the paper's own ranking rule.
